 🍳 Happy Sunday! Smart Brevity™ count: 1,380 words ... 5 mins. Thanks to Neal Rothschild for orchestrating. Edited by Andrew Pantazi. 

 📊 New this morning: Sixteen days before midterms, President Trump's approval rating sank to a record low of 33%, with 66% disapproving, in the Washington Post-ABC News-Ipsos poll. Keep reading . 

 Jump in Republicans who think Trump is enriching himself: About one-third of Republicans and GOP-leaning independents hold that view, up from about one-fifth a year ago, the poll finds. Go deeper . 🏈 Shakeup Saturday: In college football's AP Top 25 , five of the top seven teams — No. 1 Georgia, No. 2 Notre Dame, No. 5 Indiana, No. 6 Alabama and No. 7 Texas — lost on the same day in the regular season for the first time in the 90-year history of the poll. Keep reading . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Anti-party, anti-politician candidates surge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images In red states and blue, unconventional candidates are downplaying — or even disowning — party labels to tap into voters' disdain for Washington, Axios' Hans Nichols writes. 

 Why it matters: They might not all win, but the sheer number of political outsiders connecting with voters tells us something about the electorate: It's angry and willing to take risks.

 The candidates — whether they come from the pulpit or the NFL sidelines — are making the same bet: Voters are tired of career politicians. 🗺️ Zoom in: Consider three unorthodox Democrats leading Senate contests in red states: a Presbyterian seminarian in Texas (state Rep. James Talarico), a megachurch pastor in Kansas (Adam Hamilton), and an Alaska Native in that state (former Rep. Mary Peltola, who once skipped a week of House votes to go fishing when Chinook salmon were running).

 Traditionally blue Minnesota's Senate race features Michele Tafoya — a former NFL sideline reporter who's endorsed by President Trump but calls herself an independent Republican. She's mounting a serious challenge to Democrat Peggy Flanagan, the lieutenant governor. In House races, Democrats are fielding a smokejumper in Montana ( Sam Forstag), a 29-year-old barista in Colorado (Melat Kiros) and a Tejano singer in Texas (Bobby Pulido). Four independent Senate candidates are courting Democratic voters without a (D) next to their names — and sometimes without a Democrat in the race.

 In Nebraska, Dan Osborn, a union steamfitter and former factory mechanic, is mounting a serious independent challenge to Sen. Pete Ricketts, a former two-term governor whose family owns the Chicago Cubs. In Montana, Seth Bodnar, a former Army Green Beret and University of Montana president, is running as an independent for an open Senate seat. Democrats tried to persuade their nominee to drop out, but she stayed in the race. In South Dakota, Brian Bengs, a one-time park ranger, is challenging Republican Sen. Mike Rounds as an independent. Four years ago, Bengs ran as a Democrat against Sen. John Thune. In Idaho, former Army tank commander and tech executive Todd Achilles is running as an independent against Republican Sen. Jim Risch. (Achilles previously served as a Democrat in the Idaho House.) Share this story . 

 
 
