 3. 🫆 Internet loses human touch 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images The internet is undergoing a foundational shift as websites evolve from human destinations to information hubs for AI agents, Axios' Sara Fischer and Kerry Flynn write in an Axios Media Trends Executive briefing. 

 Why it matters: Human discretion, a critical safeguard for establishing trust online, is rapidly disappearing. The big picture: Bad actors are using AI to create new information sources designed to manipulate LLMs. 

 🔮 State of play: Premium publishers and brands are looking to get ahead of this problem in the short term by becoming reliable information warehouses for bots while serving premium experiences for humans.

 At TIME, a news publisher that has leaned heavily into agentic optimization, human visitors encounter articles with imagery and storytelling while AI agents receive structured information, COO Mark Howard told Axios. TIME's Agent Ads product extends that approach to advertising, providing machine-readable information about sponsors, including FAQs. Reality check: Smaller companies may struggle to handle an influx of bot traffic or build the infrastructure needed to serve both humans and machines.

 The bottom line: Few consumer websites will survive as direct destinations in the agentic era.

 Without a trust layer to vet information that machines retrieve and analyze, the internet risks becoming a breeding ground for content that's useless to humans. Share this story ... Subscribe to Axios Media Trends Executive . 

 
 
