 5. 🛰️ Russian satellite approach raises alarms 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios A Russian satellite was tracked Friday closely stalking a U.S.-owned commercial satellite — part of a space-based network that has supported Ukraine's military, Axios space expert Thomas Novelly writes. 

 Why it matters: Experts told Axios it's a rare maneuver that raises the possibility of Russian interference with U.S. space equipment. Space intelligence company LeoLabs said Friday that the Russian satellite's movement "appears to be a deliberate approach toward" the U.S.-owned imaging satellite.

 As of Friday afternoon, Russia's Cosmos 2612 satellite was roughly three miles from the American satellite, LeoLabs said. Between the lines: Aerospace analysts and legal experts said it's worrisome that Russia burned the satellite's fuel and changed its altitude, orbital plane and speed to approach the U.S. satellite.

 Clayton Swope, a CSIS senior space fellow, said on Friday the distance is essentially "bumper-to-bumper." Share this story . 

 
 
